arXiv:2507.06489v1 [cs.CL] 09 Jul 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks </h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Stephen Obadinma
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiaodan Zhu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering,
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Ingenuity Labs Research Institute, Queen’s University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{16sco, xiaodan.zhu}@queensu.ca</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Robust verbal confidence generated by large language models (LLMs) is crucial for the deployment of LLMs to ensure transparency, trust, and safety in human-AI interactions across many high-stakes applications.
In this paper, we present the first comprehensive study on the robustness of verbal confidence under adversarial attacks. We introduce a novel framework for attacking verbal confidence scores through both perturbation and jailbreak-based methods, and show that these attacks can significantly jeopardize verbal confidence estimates and lead to frequent answer changes. We examine a variety of prompting strategies, model sizes, and application domains, revealing that current confidence elicitation methods are vulnerable and that commonly used defence techniques are largely ineffective or counterproductive. Our findings underscore the urgent need to design more robust mechanisms for confidence expression in LLMs, as even subtle semantic-preserving modifications can lead to misleading confidence in responses.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the unprecedented advancement of large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> and their widespread deployment in real-world systems, the robustness in acquiring LLMs’ confidence is crucial for a wide range of applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> including high-stakes tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>. For human beings, the expression of epistemic uncertainty using natural language is an innate ability. We envision in the near future it will be crucial for AI systems to express their confidence verbally and do so accurately
to produce seamless interactions in human-AI collaborations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The ability of LLMs to verbally convey their confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, called <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verbal confidence</span> in this paper, is not only important for applications, but also an indicator of the intelligence of LLMs.
Ensuring accurate and robust verbal confidence also underlies the objective of aligning AI models’ core values with those of humans, such as
<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">honesty</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>, which could be crucial to avoiding mistrust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> and maintaining future AI to be safe.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06489v1/motivation_diagram_v2.svg" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/181;" width="229" height="181"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06489v1/attack_framework_v2.svg" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/181;" width="229" height="181"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) Demonstration of how perturbations on prompts can affect final verbal confidence (b) An overview of our proposed attack framework centered on using generated confidence scores to optimize attacks that either perturb the prompt or append a series of optimized trigger tokens that ultimately result in a reduction in confidence.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, many state-of-the-art (SOTA) LLMs do not allow direct access to model information which remains a crucial limitation for uncertainty estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>.
Verbal confidence enables the estimation of uncertainty strictly using generated outputs,
leading to a considerable amout of recent research on examining the role of verbal confidence
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>
as well as the influence and implication of verbal confidence on trustworthiness and transparency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Real-world industry systems have also utilized verbal confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>,
where analyzing LLMs’ behaviour on this front is key for successful operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our work presents the first comprehensive study on the robustness of verbal confidence under adversarial attacks and addresses the basic question: <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how robust is the verbal confidence of the existing large language models?</span>
We highlight the differences in how models perceive and manage uncertainty cues compared to how humans naturally understand them.
Furthermore, given the ease of attaining verbal confidence from even the most guarded black-box models, it is key to determine how effectively these can be leveraged by adversaries as an attack objective to alter model behaviour in other ways, such as generated answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the Achilles’ heels of LLMs models in expressing their confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>,
we propose attack models designed for attacking verbal confidence scores through both perturbation and jailbreak-based approaches, and show that these attacks can significantly jeopardize verbal confidence estimates.
We explore three key questions for model deployment that have not been explored: (i) How vulnerable are these estimates under a range of adversarial attack techniques? (ii) How can these attacks be effectively crafted to target confidence and answer changes? (iii) How easily can such attacks be defended against with existing defending methods?
Detailed discussions on real-world applications and the importance of maintaining proper confidence can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Motivation ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In brief, the main contributions of our work are summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to provide a comprehensive study of adversarial attacks on verbal confidence (as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and demonstrate their threat to generated confidences and answers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design novel verbal confidence attacks based on both perturbation-based and jailbreak-based approaches, showing they can cause up to a 30% reduction in average confidence on user queries and prompt a high rate of answer changes (up to 100% in originally correctly predicted samples).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a thorough analysis of the stability and behaviour of verbal confidence in various scenarios and under different mitigation strategies,
demonstrating that most mitigation strategies are ineffective or counterproductive against such confidence attacks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adversarial Attacks in NLP.</span>
Adversarial attacks have served as a critical tool to reveal key vulnerabilities of vision and NLP models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. Generally adversarial attack methods can be divided into those that are perturbation-based and those that are jailbreak-based. Recent works of the former type have examined the robustness of LLMs under word level attacks that target prediction and generation accuracy  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite> through threat vectors such as demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>.
Jailbreak-based attack methods involve manually or algorithmically <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> generated trigger phrases that are appended to the input prompt to bypass safety guardrails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. The first methods utilized means such as optimized universal adversarial triggers (UAT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> and top-<math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> gradient-based search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>. Recent variants of trigger attacks include the GCG attack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>, black-box only approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, and those with different optimization methods such as genetic algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, policy gradient methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and using specially trained LLMs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Vulnerability of Model Confidence.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to attacks that targeting victim models’ accuracy, adversarial attacks against models’ confidence has been understudied but has become more prominent as AI models are deployed into more applications where calibration robustness needs to investigated (see further discussion in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Motivation ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). The previous works targeted logits-based confidence, where confidence is obtained from neural networks themselves. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galil and El-Yaniv [13]</a></cite> formulated confidence attacks and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emde et al. [11]</a></cite> examined confidence attacks through certified robustness guarantees. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Obadinma et al. [43]</a></cite> designed a framework that tunes adversarial attacks to target miscalibration. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al. [70]</a></cite> examined attacks that target uncertainties derived from output tokens’ logits.
None of these approaches, however, have investigated verbal confidences, which, as discussed above, is a basic problem that needs to be further understood.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Natural-Language Confidence.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, a significant amount of research has been conducted to examine the effectiveness of explicitly generating confidence scores alongside answers (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al. [15]</a></cite> for a detailed survey).
For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. [35]</a></cite> examined fine-tuning decoder-only LLMs to generate calibrated confidence. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al. [59]</a></cite> evaluated prompting models for confidence scores and gauge the calibration accordingly. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. [66]</a></cite> examined multiple prompting, sampling, and aggregation strategies for verbal confidence scores. Our research is the first to examine the robustness of verbal confidence through adversarial attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Adversarial Attacks on Verbal Confidence</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the robustness of verbal confidence produced by LLMs. Given a user prompt consisting of a query <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{X}}\equiv\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{X}}\equiv\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with a length of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> word tokens, users of an LLM can obtain a confidence level by providing the LLM with an additional task prompt <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{P}}\equiv\{p_{1},\dots,p_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓟</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{P}}\equiv\{p_{1},\dots,p_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> word tokens, which describes the manner in which the LLM should provide a confidence score and gives an illustrative demonstration of how to do it (i.e., through an example).
The queried LLM then generates a sequence of tokens <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mathcal{Y}}\equiv\{y_{1},\ldots,y_{R}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mathcal{Y}}\equiv\{y_{1},\ldots,y_{R}\}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> word tokens as a response, containing a subsequence representing its overall answer <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\bm{A}}\subseteq\bm{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓐</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\bm{A}}\subseteq\bm{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the query. A corresponding confidence score, which we call the verbal confidence
<math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a scalar quantity attained with the help of the task-specific prompt that reflects the model’s belief in the correctness of its response <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. The score <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived strictly from the output word tokens <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> alone without any knowledge of internal model states. We refer to the entire process as <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidence elicitation</span> (CE).
Formally, confidence elicitation methods (CEMs) are functions that map a generated sequence of word tokens <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a confidence score,
<math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{CEM}:\bm{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CEM</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{CEM}:\bm{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
given a specific LLM that serves as a mapping function from the input prompts to the output sequence <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{LLM}:(\mathbf{X},\bm{\mathcal{P}})\rightarrow\bm{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{LLM}:(\mathbf{X},\bm{\mathcal{P}})\rightarrow\bm{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As detailed in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Design of Confidence Elicitation ‣ 3 Adversarial Attacks on Verbal Confidence ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, the specific CEM functions can be implemented with different prompting strategies, including <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">chain-of-thought</span>, <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-step</span>, and <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">self-consistency</span> prompting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Verbal Confidence Attack Methods</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a series of verbal confidence attack (VCA) methods to automatically generate adversarial prompts. As Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a) demonstrates, a common range of alterations in a prompt can significantly vary confidence estimates or predicted answers. This is undesirable since confidence should be robust when the meaning of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved. The framework for the proposed attacks can be seen in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b), which presents our attack objectives based on generated numeric confidence scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of our attack algorithms is to generate attacks based on an input, while maintaining semantics, that cause a reduction in verbal confidence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. For an adversarial input sequence of prompt tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{X}}=\{\hat{x}_{1},\dots,\hat{x}_{N_{ADV}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{X}}=\{\hat{x}_{1},\dots,\hat{x}_{N_{ADV}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is a constrained optimization problem with a objective: <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\mathbf{\hat{X}}}\mbox{CEM}(\mbox{LLM}(\mathbf{\hat{X}},\bm{\mathcal{P}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>CEM</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\mathbf{\hat{X}}}\mbox{CEM}(\mbox{LLM}(\mathbf{\hat{X}},\bm{\mathcal{P}}))</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Sim}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{\hat{X}})&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Sim}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{\hat{X}})&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, under a similarity function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mbox{Sim}(.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Sim</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Sim}(.)</annotation></semantics></math> and threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
Likewise, a similar formulation applies if <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is unmodified and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the adversarial input.
Unlike conventional adversarial frameworks, our objective is not to explicitly prioritize misclassifications, but rather to attack confidence and observe any consequences as a result. Unlike logit-based attacks, misclassifications are not necessarily bound to occur once top class predicted confidence is degraded enough. Nevertheless, our goal is also see to how frequently misclassifications can be induced by using VCAs given that this form of confidence is easily obtainable by adversaries. We study three threat vectors: user queries <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{\mathbf{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{\mathbf{X}})</annotation></semantics></math>, along with system prompts and one-shot demonstrations that provide task examples (which combined comprise <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Perturbation-based Confidence Attack</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose two perturbation-based verbal confidence attacks (VCA): <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VCA-TF</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VCA-TB</span>. These VCA attacks are based on
two popular black-box attack algorithms: <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Textfooler (TF)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Textbugger (TB)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. However, unlike the original algorithms, we design new scoring functions which are optimized using verbal confidence in contrast to class-wise probabilities derived from model logits. The key principal behind these methods is to generate importance scores for each token in the sequence by determining the corresponding difference in the predicted confidence without the token present in the sequence, before ranking each token and prioritizing perturbations on tokens with the highest difference. VCA-TF focuses on synonym substitutions, while VCA-TB includes character modifications like common typos.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we formulate two attack types that iteratively attempt corrupting an input through random common typos (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Typos/Ty.</span>), or through word level modifications that include random synonym substitutions, adjacent token swaps, or token removals (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SubSwapRemove/SSR</span>) to optimize the attack objective.
Detailed descriptions of these perturbation-based attacks can be found in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Details of Perturbation-based Attacks ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_figure ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:196.7pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> ConfidenceTriggers Optimization</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
