</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Jailbreak-based Confidence Attack</h3>

<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>ConfidenceTriggers</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b), we show the potential for a more generalizable form of attack by designing a VCA method called <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ConfidenceTriggers</span>, which is designed to target confidence based on a popular jailbreak approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. By optimizing a single series of trigger tokens that get appended to any number of prompts, LLMs are led to generate lowered confidences scores for these prompts. Note that our work is the first to optimize and study triggers that target verbal confidence scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Triggers are optimized using a completely black-box genetic algorithm (<span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Perturbation-based Confidence Attack ‣ 3 Adversarial Attacks on Verbal Confidence ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>). No model-specific information or tokenizers are required, corresponding to the setup that needs verbal confidence. To optimize for confidence reduction, we first generate an initial set of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> prompts consisting of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> number of random words. These words come from a list of approximately 2000 words related to the topic of uncertainty to create an initial focused search space. This initialization process is accomplished using the InitializePrompts function in the algorithm. We append the triggers to the system prompt, as we will later show it is a more effective attack vector. This also has the byproduct of if a system or API is infiltrated with such a trigger, then confidence estimates on subsequent (benign) user queries are compromised.
The loss function to be minimized is the difference in average verbal confidence (see <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">AverageConfidence</span> function) across <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> randomly sampled training examples when using the tested trigger compared to the average confidence estimated on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> randomly sampled examples without the trigger.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To enable generalizability across different CEMs, we use four different variants to estimate the loss for each trigger. To produce prompts for the next generation, first <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> elites are selected, then pairs of parent triggers (from the previous generation) are iteratively selected through top-<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tournament selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> to undergo Single Point Cross-Over <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and Random (Uniform) Mutation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> until there are <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> child prompts. For Cross-Over, a random split point is chosen and two child prompts are created whereby the first consists of the sequence from the first parent up to the split point, and then from the second parent after the split point, and vice versa for the second child. For Mutation, a third child is created by iteratively sampling token randomly from either the first parent or second parent. 20% of indices are chosen as mutation points, where a random word is sampled instead. After <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> generations, the lowest loss prompt is selected. The efficiency of this attack algorithm is dependent on the input parameters in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Perturbation-based Confidence Attack ‣ 3 Adversarial Attacks on Verbal Confidence ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with the equation for number of model calls needed to optimize a set of triggers being approximately <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{Z}|*S+G*\xi*|Z|*\alpha+\alpha*|\mathbf{Z}|*S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{Z}|*S+G*\xi*|Z|*\alpha+\alpha*|\mathbf{Z}|*S</annotation></semantics></math>, although once optimized a set of tokens can be reused indefinitely. Detailed descriptions of settings and the Cross-Over and Mutation functions can be found in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 ConfidenceTriggers Settings ‣ Appendix C Detailed Experimental Settings ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>ConfidenceTriggers-AutoDAN</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As an alternative method for more naturalistic triggers, we propose a variant of ConfidenceTriggers based on AutoDAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> (<span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ConfidenceTriggers-AutoDAN</span>). AutoDAN is an algorithm for automatically generating stealthy
jailbreak prompts through a hierarchical genetic algorithm. Featuring the same structure as ConfidenceTriggers, hierarchical perturbations are produced each generation at the sentence-level through synonym replacement based on scores from a momentum word score dictionary, and at the paragraph-level using GPT4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> to rephrase pairs of parent prompts for Cross-Over and Mutation. Full details and the algorithm are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D ConfidenceTriggers-AutoDAN Details ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Design of Confidence Elicitation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our confidence elicitation methods are based on the framework proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, where CEMs primarily consist of a prompting strategy (i.e., task instruction) for generating verbalized numeric confidence scores. CEMs can also include sampling strategies for generating multiple LLM responses, which are then aggregated to ideally produce a more representative confidence. We use the self-random sampling strategy, which samples <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> different output sequences <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{\mathcal{Y}}_{1},\bm{\mathcal{Y}}_{2},\dots,\bm{\mathcal{Y}}_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓨</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{\mathcal{Y}}_{1},\bm{\mathcal{Y}}_{2},\dots,\bm{\mathcal{Y}}_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> using a specific temperature parameter, from which confidence estimates <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{C}_{1},\dots\mathcal{C}_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{C}_{1},\dots\mathcal{C}_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are derived. To aggregate these disparate estimates, we take the average of the confidence estimates over the <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> estimates as the final confidence estimate <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{AVG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{AVG}</annotation></semantics></math>, and choose the most common predicted label as the answer <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{FINAL}=\mbox{Mode}(\mathcal{A}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{A}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Mode</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{FINAL}=\mbox{Mode}(\mathcal{A}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{A}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As described in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Design of Confidence Elicitation ‣ 3 Adversarial Attacks on Verbal Confidence ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we propose to incorporate four CEMs: <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Base</span>, <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chain-of-Thought (CoT)</span>, <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Step (MS)</span>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Averaged CoT</span> (Self Consistency Prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> or SC).
The first three methods are based on deterministic sampling. SC allows us to assess attack effectiveness under more random variation. We note that MS produces far less confident responses allowing us to ascertain attack performance in lower confidence scenarios. We focus on these CEMs since they are the most straightforward to attain and directly capture the model’s perception of confidence with minimal processing. Examples of prompts can be found in Appendix <a href="#A20" title="Appendix T Prompt Examples ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">T</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:57%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Description of CEMs.</figcaption>
