</figure>
<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We examine if CEMs are robust against generic confidence-agnostic adversarial attacks.
To this end, we compare the confidences generated for the original instances in SST-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> and their AdvGLUE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> versions since these comprehensive adversarial modifications were solely optimized for misclassifications.
The results are in Table <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix E Confidence Agnostic Attacks ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, where the difference in confidences are minor, with at most a 5% drop despite a high percentage of samples being effected.
CEMs are shown to be generally robust against confidence-agnostic perturbations, even though they are effective in reducing accuracy by 15-20%.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Full Results Tables</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The full results for the system prompt and demonstration attacks are in Table <a href="#A6.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix F Full Results Tables ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Likwise, the full results for attacks against Llama-3-70B and GPT-4o are in Table <a href="#A6.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix F Full Results Tables ‣ On the Robustness of Verbal Confidence of LLMs in Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
